Try another access point after one doesn't answer.

When an access point doesn't answer authentication or association,
wpa_supplicant calls wpas_connection_failed() and only then marks the
interface disconnected. On a first failure that function picks a BSS from
the last scan straight away. With more than one network enabled, the ignore
list lets the BSS just tried be picked again, and wpa_supplicant_connect()
sees the interface still authenticating with that very BSS, says "Already
associated or trying to connect with the selected AP", and does nothing.
The pick still counts as done, so no scan is asked for either. The caller
then marks the interface disconnected, and it stays that way, with nothing
pending, until something outside asks it to look or join.

This picks from the last scan once the caller has finished, from the event
loop, so the attempt is over when the pick is made. Where nothing can be
picked, it asks for a scan just as a later failure does.

Where the last scan heard another BSS of the same ESS, the pick skips the
one that just failed rather than trying it once more. Some access points
ignore a station's first authentication after it boots, and trying the same
one again fails too, after which a full scan, about six seconds on a card
with 6 GHz, comes before its neighbour is tried.
